--- a/packages/context/agent-instructions/README.md
+++ b/packages/context/agent-instructions/README.md
@@ -37,6 +37,8 @@ The first request includes one durable baseline message with the user-global fil
 
 A line containing `@path` imports another file, as in Claude Code's `CLAUDE.md`. The path may start with `~/`, `/`, `./` or `../`, or be a relative path ending in a file extension; it resolves against the importing file's directory. The reference line stays and the imported content follows it under `Imported from <path>:`. Imports nest up to five levels; a file already imported while expanding one instruction file (a cycle, or the same file named again) is not read or shown again, and references inside fenced or inline code, e-mail addresses, and missing or unreadable files are left as written. A project instruction file may only import files inside its project root, checked on the real path so a symlink cannot escape; only the real user-global file (`AGENTS.md` in the harness home, recognized by absolute path, not by how the path is displayed) may import any readable file. An import of a file discovery loads anyway (`AGENTS.md` or `AGENTS.local.md` in the importing folder or above it) is skipped; `@CLAUDE.md` is not discovered, so it expands like any other file. `maxSourceBytes` bounds a file and its imports together and is enforced while reading, so expansion stops before the first import that would exceed it, and one load probes or reads at most 200 imported files. Imported files are read when their importing file loads or reloads, so editing only an imported file takes effect in the next session.
 
+When the sandbox policy hides secret files (`protectSecrets`), imports skip them too, whoever imports them: a user-global `@~/.ssh/id_ed25519` or `@../other-project/.env` stays a reference and its content never reaches the model.
+
 ### Configuration
 
 The defaults suit a typical checkout: `.git` marks the project root, `AGENTS.md` is the base candidate, and `AGENTS.local.md` is the additive local overlay. Only `maxBytes` is required — it caps the complete rendered baseline so each deployment chooses its prompt budget explicitly.
